Liquid Glass is only one layer of Apple’s rendering stack. What actually
separates native Apple interfaces from third-party lookalikes is a coordinated
system of ~11 additional optical, geometric, typographic, display, and physics
subsystems running across QuartzCore, CoreGraphics, RenderBox, CoreBrightness,
CoreHaptics, and BackBoard / WindowServer.

Below is the complete OSINT engineering catalog—the internals, exact equations,
constants, conditions, and recipes—for everything besides Liquid Glass.

1. Legacy Material Engine (NSVisualEffectView / UIVisualEffectView & CoreUI)

Even in apps using traditional blur materials (and in every control inside
sheets/sidebars before iOS 26), copycats just apply Gaussian blur + alpha tint.
Reverse-engineering of NSVisualEffectView and .car (CoreUI) asset catalogs shows
Apple’s exact recipe:

Internal Layer Tree & CABackdropLayer Settings

VisualEffectView.layer
└─ container (CALayer, masksToBounds = true)
   ├─ backdrop (CABackdropLayer)
   └─ tint (CALayer, compositingFilter = blendMode)

  - Performance & Artifact Prevention:
      - backdrop.scale = 0.25 (quarter-resolution downsampling prior to blur)
      - backdrop.bleedAmount = 10.0 (extends sampling 10pt beyond clipped bounds
        so edge blur kernels don't darken/clamp at borders)
      - backdrop.allowsInPlaceFiltering = false (prevents stale/laggy buffer
        reuse)
      - backdrop.groupName = sharedUUID (syncs adjacent materials so seams don't
        appear)
      - blur.setValue(true, forKey: "inputNormalizeEdges")
  - WindowServer Anti-Flattening (macOS behind-window blending):
      - WindowServer flattens layer trees into static bitmaps after ~1s of idle
        or during Spaces/Mission Control swipes, killing live blur. Apple
        bypasses this with:
          - shouldAutoFlattenLayerTree = false
          - CGSSetWindowTags(..., kCGSNeverFlattenSurfacesDuringSwipesTagBit) (1
            << 16, re-applied on windowDidEndLiveResize)
          - backdrop.allowsSubstituteColor = true (substitutes neutral color
            over DRM/secure fields instead of black)

Exact Filter Order & Math (CoreUI)

Strict pipeline order: ColorSaturate \to GaussianBlur \to ColorBrightness \to
Tint (CompositingFilter).

  - Standard Recipe Anchor (e.g., sidebar / Control Center base): blurRadius
    = 30.0, saturation = 1.8, brightness = 0.02.
  - Light Mode Blend: Base lift + darkenBlendMode (min(backdrop, tint)) or
    "plusD" (Plus Darker) to prevent white blowouts.
  - Dark Mode Blend: Base clamp + lightenBlendMode (max(backdrop, tint)) or
    "plusL" (Plus Lighter) to prevent black holes.
  - Vibrancy (UIVibrancyEffect / vibrantLight / vibrantDark /
    vibrantColorMatrix): Text/icons over blurred glass do not use flat alpha
    opacity. They run CAFilter("vibrantColorMatrix") with inputBackdropAware =
    true (4×5 RGBA matrix + hue-preserving clamp inputClampPreserveHue) so
    foreground glyphs selectively invert/amplify relative to the filtered
    backdrop.

2. Continuous Corners (kCACornerCurveContinuous) & Hardware Concentricity

CSS border-radius creates a G^1 circular arc where curvature \kappa jumps
instantaneously from 0 \to 1/r at the tangent point, causing highlights and
shadows to look "bolted on".

A. QuartzCore's Real Shader (supercircle_sdf) vs. CGPath

  - Screen Shader (QuartzCore supercircle_sdf): Does not use a global
    superellipse |x|^n+|y|^n=1. Instead, it takes a quarter-circle around the
    45^\circ diagonal whose curvature tapers smoothly to zero along a quartic
    ratio in the corner box, meeting the straight edge at 1.528665 \cdot r from
    the geometric corner.
      - Short-side / Pill Fallback: When a side length L is too short for both
        corners (L < 2 \times 1.528665\,r), Core Animation smoothly blends the
        curve back toward a true quarter-circle using a clamp factor derived
        from L / r_{\text{mean}} (so a circle/capsule degenerates cleanly into
        circular arcs).
      - Shader Anti-Aliasing: Edge coverage is computed analytically via
        gradient magnitude:
        \alpha = \text{clamp}(0.5 - d / (|\partial_x d| + |\partial_y d|), 0, 1).
  - Exact 3-Bézier CGPath Normalized Constants (Zero-Pixel-Error Replica for 1
    corner, scaled by r): Starting on top edge at (1.528665\,r,\; 0) moving into
    the corner (0,0):
    1.  Cubic 1: c1 = (1.08849296, 0), c2 = (0.86840694, 0), end =
        (0.63149379, 0.07491139) (scaled by r)
    2.  Central / Transitional Cubic(s) & Mirror: using the extracted normalized
        constants
        [1.528665, 1.08849296, 0.86840694, 0.63149379, 0.07491139, 0.37282383, 0.16905956, 0.0].
        (Tip: For custom SDF renderers free of Apple's tiny legacy asymmetry
        artifact, use a Walton–Meek clothoid spline or L_4 superellipse
        |x/a|^4 + |y/b|^4 = 1 with \kappa(\pm a, 0) = 0.)

B. Concentricity (ConcentricRectangle / _displayCornerRadius)

Sheets, widgets, notifications, and cards nest concentrically with the physical
display bezel (UIScreen._displayCornerRadius, e.g., ~55pt on Pro iPhones):
r_{\text{inner}, i} = \max\!\big(r_{\text{container}, i} - d_{\text{corner}, i},\;\; r_{\min}\big)
where d_{\text{corner}, i} is the offset from corner i of the child frame to
corner i of the enclosing containerShape / hardware screen.

  - Condition: When d_{\text{corner}, i} \ge r_{\text{container}, i} (e.g., the
    top corners of a bottom sheet far from the top bezel), the calculated
    concentric radius collapses to 0 unless explicitly overridden with a fixed
    top radius.

3. Physics Engine: Springs, Scroll Deceleration, Rubber-Banding & Retargeting

Almost every clone feels "janky" or "webby" because developers animate with CSS
ease-in-out or reset position/velocity when interrupted.

A. Apple’s Exact Spring(duration:bounce:) \leftrightarrow ODE Mapping

Apple models all UI motion as a second-order spring-mass-damper
(m \ddot{x} + c \dot{x} + k x = 0) with m = 1:
\omega_0 = \frac{2\pi}{\text{duration}}, \qquad k = \omega_0^2 = \left(\frac{2\pi}{\text{duration}}\right)^2
\zeta = \begin{cases} 1 - \text{bounce} & \text{if } \text{bounce} \ge 0 \quad (\text{underdamped / critical}) \\ \dfrac{1}{1 + \text{bounce}} & \text{if } \text{bounce} < 0 \quad (\text{overdamped}) \end{cases}
c = 2\zeta \sqrt{k} = 2\zeta\,\omega_0 (Note: \text{duration} is perceptual
duration, not settling time! Wait for both |x - x_{\text{target}}| < \epsilon_x
and |\dot{x}| < \epsilon_v before snapping to rest.)

  - Built-in System Regimes:
      - Default SwiftUI Spring: response = 0.50, dampingFraction = 0.825
        (\zeta = 0.825)
      - .smooth: \text{bounce} = 0 (\zeta = 1.0, critically damped—zero
        overshoot for navigation pushes)
      - .snappy: small positive bounce (e.g. duration = 0.28, bounce = 0.12 for
        toggles/controls)
      - .bouncy: higher positive bounce (\text{bounce} \approx 0.30)
  - Two Interruption Rules (Critical!):
    1.  Velocity-Preserving Merge (Springs): When target x_{\text{target}}
        changes mid-flight, do not start a new curve from v=0. Mutate
        target/parameters on the live ODE keeping (x, \dot{x}) continuous
        (shouldMerge = true).
    2.  Additive Composition (Timing Curves): Non-mergeable curves add relative
        delta animations (oldTarget -> newTarget) onto the presentation layer
        (iOS 8+ additive Core Animation).
    3.  Gesture-to-Spring Handoff: Drive drag updates via .interactiveSpring
        (very short response) and on finger-up merge directly into the resting
        spring—velocity transfers automatically without noisy finite-difference
        estimates.

B. UIScrollView Exact Scroll & Rubber-Band Equations

Reverse-engineered and confirmed bit-for-bit against UIKit:

  - Deceleration Velocity Curve (t in milliseconds):
    v(t) = v_0 \cdot r^{t_{\text{ms}}}, \qquad r_{\text{normal}} = 0.998\text{/ms}, \quad r_{\text{fast}} = 0.990\text{/ms}
  - Fling Snap Projection (WWDC18 803):
    x_{\text{projected}} = x_{\text{release}} + \frac{v_0}{1000} \cdot \frac{r}{1 - r}
    (At v_0 = 1000\text{ pt/s} and r = 0.998, scroll projects
    \approx 499\text{ pt}; at r = 0.990, \approx 99\text{ pt}. Snap
    pagers/carousels using x_{\text{projected}}, never x_{\text{release}}!)
  - Rubber-Band Resistance:
    b(x) = \left(1 - \frac{1}{\frac{x \cdot c}{d} + 1}\right) \cdot d, \qquad c = 0.55
    where x is linear finger overscroll past boundary and d is viewport
    dimension (e.g., on an 844\text{pt} screen, 100\text{pt} overdrag
    \to 51.6\text{pt} visual displacement; 400\text{pt} \to 174.5\text{pt}).
  - Swipe Hysteresis: \approx 10\text{ pt} touch slop before locking scroll
    direction.

4. ProMotion Frame Pacing, targetPresentationTimestamp & RenderServer Split

A. Discrete VRR Frequency Grid (Not Arbitrary Continuous Hz)

LTPO ProMotion displays on Apple devices step strictly along a 4.17\text{ ms}
scanout grid using exact discrete rates:
\{120,\; 80,\; 60,\; 48,\; 40,\; 30,\; 24,\; 20,\; 16,\; 15,\; 12,\; 10,\; 1\}\text{ Hz}
(Notice: No 90 Hz or 100 Hz bucket exists. Rendering at ~118 fps oscillates
between 8.33 ms and 12.5 ms buckets, looking worse than holding a steady 80 Hz
(12.5 ms).)

  - Scroll Velocity Step-Down: As a flick decelerates, iOS dynamically steps 120
    Hz -> 80 Hz -> 60 Hz -> 48 Hz -> 40 Hz ... -> 10 Hz (and 1 Hz on Always-On
    Display).
  - Golden Pacing Rules:
      - Require CADisableMinimumFrameDurationOnPhone = true on iOS.
      - Advance physics/shaders by targetPresentationTimestamp -
        previousPresentationTimestamp (CAMetalDisplayLink), never wall-clock
        callback time.
      - On macOS VRR, use presentAfterMinimumDuration / presentAtTime rather
        than drawable back-pressure.
      - Whenever GPU content moves alongside native OS chrome/glass, swap
        IOSurface contents or set CAMetalLayer.presentsWithTransaction = true
        inside the same CATransaction so Metal and CA never drift by 1 frame.

B. Out-of-Process Immunity (backboardd / WindowServer)

Core Animation commits layer trees across Mach IPC to an independent render
server process. Built-in SwiftUI animations evaluate springs off the main thread
directly into presentation layers—even if app layout code stalls for 200ms,
running motion stays locked at 120 Hz.

5. Variable / Progressive Blur & Scroll Edge Effects (CAVariableBlur)

Hard clipping scrolled content under a navbar or status bar instantly exposes a
third-party app. Apple uses graduated/variable blur + alpha feathering:

  - Native Primitive (CAFilter("variableBlur")): Inputs: inputMaskImage (alpha
    value per pixel scales local blur radius
    r(x,y) = \alpha_{\text{mask}}(x,y)\cdot R_{\max}), inputRadius,
    inputNormalizeEdges, inputNormalizeEdgesTransparent, inputFade, inputDither,
    inputSourceSublayerName.
  - Implementation Math (Fast Dual-Pyramid Interpolation): Since spatially
    varying Gaussian kernels are O(N^2) per pixel, build a mip/Gaussian pyramid
    L_0, L_1, L_2, \dots, L_k (at radii 0, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32\text{ px}), compute
    continuous mip level
    \lambda(\mathbf{p}) = \log_2(1 + \alpha_{\text{mask}}(\mathbf{p}) \cdot R_{\max}),
    and trilinearly interpolate between \lfloor\lambda\rfloor and
    \lceil\lambda\rceil + apply subtle blue-noise/IGN dithering (inputDither =
    true) to kill gradient banding.
  - iOS 26+ .scrollEdgeEffectStyle(.soft): Combines a progressive variableBlur
    fade mask over ~24–48pt above/below toolbars with subtle lensing so
    scrolling text dissolves into the bar instead of slicing against a line.

6. Typography: Optical Sizing, Tracking Curve, Stem Darkening & Linear Alpha Blending

Most clones load Inter or SF Pro at a fixed size/tracking and blend glyph
coverage in gamma-encoded sRGB space—producing thin, spindly white text on dark
backgrounds and muddy halos on colored backgrounds.

A. SF Pro Optical Sizing & Tracking Ladder

  - Continuous opsz Cutover: System font dynamically interpolates letterform
    geometry (wider apertures, taller x-height, looser spacing below 20pt;
    tighter proportions above): SF Pro Text \le 19\text{pt}, SF Pro Display
    \ge 20\text{pt}.
  - Optical Tracking Curve (iOS/iPadOS points):
      - 6pt: +0.24pt (+41/1000 em), 8pt: +0.21pt, 10pt: +0.12pt, 12pt: 0.00pt
      - Tightens to minimum around 17–20pt (-0.43pt at 17pt, ~ -0.45pt at
        19–20pt)
      - Rebounds positive around 24–34pt (+0.40pt at 29–34pt) before decaying
        back toward zero at giant hero sizes.
  - Dynamic Type Nonlinearity: Body scales 17pt -> 53pt (3.1x from L to AX5),
    whereas Footnote/Caption clamp at 11–12pt on the low end (xS–M) and grow
    aggressively at AX1–AX5. Never use weights below Regular in UI chrome.

B. Stem Darkening + Gamma-Correct Linear Glyph Blending

This is the hidden reason macOS/iOS text looks razor-sharp yet rich:

1.  The Problem: If you blend anti-aliased coverage \alpha \in [0, 1] directly
    in gamma-encoded sRGB (\gamma \approx 2.2), mid-grey coverage edges come out
    too dark on light backgrounds (making black text look heavier/fuzzier) and
    too thin on dark backgrounds. Conversely, if you blend physically accurately
    in linear light, thin stems visually thin out ("frail" glyphs).
2.  Apple's Two-Part Solution (CoreGraphics / CFF Rasterizer):
      - Step 1 (Pre-Rasterization Stem Darkening): At small pixel-per-em sizes,
        the rasterizer algorithmically dilates/emboldens glyph outlines by a
        small pixel fraction inversely proportional to ppem, increasing pixel
        coverage on fine stems. (FreeType’s CFF engine ported Adobe/Apple’s
        exact stem-darkening parameters for this.)
      - Step 2 (Linear-Light Blending + Re-Encoding): Convert foreground text
        color and backdrop pixel to linear light, alpha-composite in linear
        space
        (\mathbf{C}_{\text{lin}} = \alpha\,\mathbf{F}_{\text{lin}} + (1 - \alpha)\mathbf{B}_{\text{lin}}),
        then re-encode with display gamma.
      - Dark Mode Irradiation Compensation: White text on dark backgrounds
        blooms perceptually on OLEDs (irradiation illusion). Apple compensates
        via CGFont / variable font grade/dilation tuning and by avoiding #FFFFFF
        on pure #000000 for body copy where elevated dark surfaces (#1C1C1E) are
        used.

7. Display Color Pipeline: EDR Headroom, OLED Protection & True Tone (CoreBrightness)

A. Extended Dynamic Range (EDR) UI & Highlights

Apple UI elements (Apple Intelligence glows, Photos HDR thumbnails, Siri bursts,
specular highlights) exceed 1.0 SDR white:

  - Pipeline Setup: CAMetalLayer.wantsExtendedDynamicRangeContent = true,
    pixelFormat = .rgba16Float, colorspace = .extendedLinearDisplayP3 (or
    .itur_2020).
  - Headroom Math:
    \text{Headroom} = \frac{L_{\text{peak\_display}}}{L_{\text{current\_SDR\_white}}}
    Query NSScreen.maximumExtendedDynamicRangeColorComponentValue
    (currentEDRHeadroom) per frame (potentialEDRHeadroom on iOS when probing max
    capability). Scale bright glow emissive RGB by [1.0 ... currentEDRHeadroom],
    and remember iOS throttles currentEDRHeadroom -> 1.0 over 20–40 min under
    thermal load.
  - QuartzCore Built-in Display Filters:
      - CAFilter("edrGain") / CAFilter("edrGainMultiply") (inputScale,
        inputAdaptive, inputAllowsGroup)
      - CAFilter("sdrNormalize") (inputClamp, inputClampPreserveHue)
      - CAFilter("limitAveragePixelLuminance") (inputAmount, inputScale,
        inputUseP3Space, inputDisplayInvertAware) — Apple's hardware-friendly
        OLED Average Pixel Luminance (ABL) limiter to prevent abrupt screen
        dimming and burn-in.

B. True Tone White-Point Adaptation (CoreBrightness)

True Tone prevents white glass/paper UI from looking shockingly blue under warm
indoor bulbs:

1.  Multi-channel ambient light sensor measures incident spectrum \to estimates
    ambient CIE xy / CCT + D_{uv}.
2.  Applies a Bradford Linear Chromatic Adaptation Transform (CAT) (improved von
    Kries cone-response matrix M_{\text{BFD}}) converting scene D65 linear XYZ
    to target ambient white point \text{XYZ}_{\text{amb}}:
    M_{\text{CAT}} = M_{\text{BFD}}^{-1} \cdot \text{diag}\!\left(\frac{L_{\text{dst}}}{L_{\text{src}}},\; \frac{M_{\text{dst}}}{M_{\text{src}}},\; \frac{S_{\text{dst}}}{S_{\text{src}}}\right) \cdot M_{\text{BFD}}
    Notice Apple uses mixed adaptation (partial adaptation factor \alpha < 1,
    typically ~40–60% toward ambient white rather than 100%, because eyes adapt
    simultaneously to room light and the screen).

8. MeshGradient Bicubic Coons Patches & Fragment Color Evaluation

Reverse-engineering of RenderBox binaries on iOS 18–26 (KHMeshGradient Metal
research) revealed why Apple's MeshGradient looks silky smooth at 120 Hz without
triangle faceting:

1.  Separated Position & Color Surfaces: Each (u,v) \in [0,1]^2 patch builds two
    independent 16-control bicubic nets:
      - Position Net (positionNet[16]): Constructed as a Coons patch from the 4
        cubic boundary Béziers (leading, top, trailing, bottom handles) minus
        the bilinear corner blend so corners aren't double-counted.
      - Color Net (colorNet[16]): Derived via Catmull-Rom / finite-difference
        cubic tangents across neighboring vertex colors in premultiplied alpha
        (smoothsColors = true).
2.  Vertex Stage (Coarse Adaptive Grid) vs. Fragment Stage (Exact Color):
      - Tessellation density per patch axis is chosen on CPU as a power of two
        (n \in \{1, 4, 8, 16, \dots\}) from screen-space second differences
        (bound > targetPx * n^2)—a flat 4-corner mesh uses just 2 triangles (n
        = 1)!
      - Color is evaluated per-pixel in the fragment shader (never
        vertex-interpolated!), using FMA power-basis along v (fma(fma(fma(c12,
        v, c8), v, c4), v, c0)) to cut ALU instructions and Bernstein basis
        along u ((1-u)^3, 3(1-u)^2u, 3(1-u)u^2, u^3) to avoid floating-point
        cancellation—using half4 for opaque sRGB pipelines.
      - Anti-Banding: Add 1\text{ LSB} triangular-PDF dither before 8-bit
        quantization.

